;; Reviewed 2026-10-11 by the user (written by Claude, from design
;; section 14, item 9, the VAX architecture's floating point and the
;; guest runs). Why this is right:
;; - Case 9 divides the REAL 1.0 by 0.0. The VAX has no infinity and no
;;   NaN, so DIVF is a floating divide-by-zero fault (FLTDIV_F) before
;;   anything is printed, where LLVM's IEEE quotient is an infinity and
;;   the program goes on (language-extensions.md, "Overflow, division
;;   and reals"; as llvm-entier-trap's case 5 on the VAX).
;; - VMS's own message, then its traceback: the module's code, called
;;   from the program's start; tools/vax-suite masks the addresses.
;; - The same under -O2 and -OC.
;; - Nothing is printed before the fault, so VMS's message and
;;   traceback are in standard output too (llvm-entier-trap, 2026-10-10).
%SYSTEM-F-FLTDIV_F, arithmetic fault, floating divide by zero at PC=xxxxxxxx, PSL=xxxxxxxx
%TRACE-F-TRACEBACK, symbolic stack dump follows
module name     routine name                     line       rel PC    abs PC

NOTRAP          POC_CODE                                   xxxxxxxx  xxxxxxxx
NOTRAP          NOTRAP_MAIN                                xxxxxxxx  xxxxxxxx
